{∘}}=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${135}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{135}^{∘}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡{135}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{135}^{∘}}=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354465 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $cos⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle cos⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354466 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{-\\frac{1}{2}}=-2.\n$$" "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{-\\frac{1}{2}}=-2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354487 :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}=1.$" "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354488 :question/problem "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}=1.$" "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354507 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354523 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot 1}{(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $b=5,$ $c=-2,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{b}=\\frac{-2}{5}=-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$" "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot 1}{(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $b=5,$ $c=-2,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{b}=\\frac{-2}{5}=-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354524 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 5+\\sqrt{7}\\cdot 0}{(-1)^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{bmatrix}}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=\\frac{5}{2},$ $b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 5+\\sqrt{7}\\cdot 0}{(-1)^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=\\frac{5}{2},$ $b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354525 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{0\\cdot \\sqrt{2}+(-5)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-3)}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $b=-\\frac{13}{3},$ $c=-1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{b}=\\frac{-1}{\\left(-\\frac{13}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{3}{13}.\n$$" "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{0\\cdot \\sqrt{2}+(-5)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-3)}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $b=-\\frac{13}{3},$ $c=-1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{b}=\\frac{-1}{\\left(-\\frac{13}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{3}{13}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354526 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot 2+5\\cdot (-1)}{(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{-3\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{-3\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5},$ $c=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\,0\\,|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}|+|\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}|=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\n$$" "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot 2+5\\cdot (-1)}{(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{-3\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{-3\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5},$ $c=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\,0\\,|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}|+|\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}|=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354527 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (0)}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, $B={{u}_{1},{u}_{2}}={\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ $c=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}|=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (0)}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B={{u}_{1},{u}_{2}}={\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ $c=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}|=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354530 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/62/62925844de27c9731b181f17a1bdd912bfa3265176bd62ebda425d182ae8d9e8.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not completely cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{R}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,2]$” is true. Indeed, the rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,2]$” only." "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/62/62925844de27c9731b181f17a1bdd912bfa3265176bd62ebda425d182ae8d9e8.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not completely cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,2]$” is true. Indeed, the rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,2]$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186354532 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/23/23f31619eda46f1e8604695c4056d4c0c5bb8ee1e186b1316151762d5b5c8e59.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$ and $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$ equal $\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(0,1)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$; $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$ only." "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/23/23f31619eda46f1e8604695c4056d4c0c5bb8ee1e186b1316151762d5b5c8e59.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\n$$ equal $\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}.\n$$ The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(0,1)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned};\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186354533 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/f3/f3ffc82025f80c357c06e28fcb091c701d53d84821bebd9d2886cf4972b77028.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal ${x}^{5}-{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain  \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{5}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions $\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$ and $\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$ equal ${x}^{5}-{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function $\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,0)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$; $\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$ only." "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/f3/f3ffc82025f80c357c06e28fcb091c701d53d84821bebd9d2886cf4972b77028.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal ${x}^{5}-{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain  \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{5}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\n$$ equal ${x}^{5}-{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}.\n$$ The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,0)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned};\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186354536 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{10}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{10}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{10}k{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{k}{x}]}_{2}^{10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{10}+\\frac{k}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2k}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2k}{5}=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{10}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{10}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{10}k{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{k}{x}]}_{2}^{10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{10}+\\frac{k}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2k}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2k}{5}=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354537 :question/problem "Given a data set with $20$ data points and ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{x}_{i}=169,$ compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." "Given a data set with $20$ data points and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{x}_{i}=169,\n$$ compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place."] [:db/cas 17592186354537 :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=20$ and ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{x}_{i}=169,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{20}\\cdot 169≈8.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have rounded our answer to one decimal place." "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=20$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{20}{x}_{i}=169,\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{20}\\cdot 169≈8.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have rounded our answer to one decimal place."] [:db/cas 17592186354538 :question/problem "Given a data set with $23$ data points and ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{23}{x}_{i}=590,$ compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." "Given a data set with $23$ data points and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{23}{x}_{i}=590,\n$$ compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place."] [:db/cas 17592186354538 :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=23$ and ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{23}{x}_{i}=590,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{23}\\cdot 590≈25.7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have rounded our answer to one decimal place." "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=23$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{23}{x}_{i}=590,\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{23}\\cdot 590≈25.7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have rounded our answer to one decimal place."] [:db/cas 17592186354557 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{k}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{k}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}k{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{k}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{72}+\\frac{k}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{24}=1\\,⟹\\,k=24.\n$$" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{k}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{k}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}k{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{k}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{72}+\\frac{k}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{24}=1\\,⟹\\,k=24.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354558 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{-5x}$ on the interval $0\\le x<\\infty ,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x={[-\\frac{k}{5}{e}^{-5x}]}_{0}^{\\infty }=\\frac{k}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{5}=1\\,⟹\\,k=5.\n$$" "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{-5x}$ on the interval $0\\le x<\\infty ,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x={[-\\frac{k}{5}{e}^{-5x}]}_{0}^{\\infty }=\\frac{k}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{5}=1\\,⟹\\,k=5.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354559 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{6x}$ on the interval $-\\infty <x\\le 0,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}k{e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}k{e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x={[\\frac{k}{6}{e}^{6x}]}_{-\\infty }^{0}=\\frac{k}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{6}=1\\,⟹\\,k=6.\n$$" "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{6x}$ on the interval $-\\infty <x\\le 0,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}k{e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}k{e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x={[\\frac{k}{6}{e}^{6x}]}_{-\\infty }^{0}=\\frac{k}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{6}=1\\,⟹\\,k=6.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354560 :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=15$ points in our data set, and we know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{x}_{i}=-30,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}\\cdot (-30) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{x}_{i}^{2}=120,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}\\cdot 120 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =8-{(-2)}^{2} \\\\  & =8-4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=15$ points in our data set, and we know that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{x}_{i}=-30,\n$$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}\\cdot (-30) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{x}_{i}^{2}=120,\n$$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}\\cdot 120 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =8-{(-2)}^{2} \\\\  & =8-4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186354569 :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ is the mean of the data set.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the mean. There are $n=6$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (3+1+15+3+2+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 36 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[(3-6)^{2}+(1-6)^{2}+(15-6)^{2}+(3-6)^{2}+(2-6)^{2}+(12-6)^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[9+25+81+9+16+36] \\\\  & =\\frac{176}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{88}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle \\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the mean of the data set.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the mean. There are $n=6$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (3+1+15+3+2+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 36 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[(3-6)^{2}+(1-6)^{2}+(15-6)^{2}+(3-6)^{2}+(2-6)^{2}+(12-6)^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[9+25+81+9+16+36] \\\\  & =\\frac{17