erential equation $y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}-4{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x?$" "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}-4{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186360730 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{36{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{36{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186360731 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+36}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+36}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186360816 :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )).$*" "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )).\n$$*"] [:db/cas 17592186362269 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{2}^{3}[f(t)-3g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t$ \ngiven that \n${\\int }_{2}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨2,\\,-4,\\,-2⟩$\nand \n${\\int }_{2}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩.$" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{3}[f(t)-3g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ \ngiven that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨2,\\,-4,\\,-2⟩\n$$\nand \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362270 :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{2}[2f(t)+3g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t$ given that ${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨{e}^{2}-1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2,\\,3⟩$ and ${\\int }_{0}^{2}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨3,\\,-1,\\,5⟩.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}[2f(t)+3g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨{e}^{2}-1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2,\\,3⟩\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨3,\\,-1,\\,5⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362274 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)+⟨2,\\,1,\\,3⟩\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{2}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-4,\\,-5,\\,1⟩.$" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)+⟨2,\\,1,\\,3⟩\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\ngiven that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-4,\\,-5,\\,1⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362275 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}f(t)-⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{2}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨2,\\,-4,\\,6⟩.$" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}f(t)-⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\ngiven that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨2,\\,-4,\\,6⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362276 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{2}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{2}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨5,\\,-1,\\,2⟩$\nand\n${\\int }_{3}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-2,\\,-4,\\,3⟩.$" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\ngiven that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨5,\\,-1,\\,2⟩\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-2,\\,-4,\\,3⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362277 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{3}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{3}^{6}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨8,\\,-6,\\,15⟩$\nand\n${\\int }_{4}^{6}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-2,\\,3,\\,4⟩.$" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\ngiven that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{6}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨8,\\,-6,\\,15⟩\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{4}^{6}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-2,\\,3,\\,4⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362280 :question/problem "Find ${\\int }_{2}^{3}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ \ngiven that \n${\\int }_{2}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=6\\,i-4\\,j+3\\,k$ \nand \n$c=i-2\\,j+2\\,k.$" "Find $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{3}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ \ngiven that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=6\\,i-4\\,j+3\\,k\n$$ \nand \n$c=i-2\\,j+2\\,k.$"] [:db/cas 17592186362311 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{6}{x(x-2)}=\\frac{3}{x}.$" "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{x-2}-\\frac{6}{x(x-2)}=\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362312 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{w}+\\frac{3}{w(w-3)}=\\frac{1}{w-3}.$" "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{w}+\\frac{3}{w(w-3)}=\\frac{1}{w-3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362320 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{1}{x+2}=\\frac{4}{x(x+2)}.$" "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{1}{x+2}=\\frac{4}{x(x+2)}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362321 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{N+1}-\\frac{1}{{N}^{2}+N}=\\frac{1}{N}.$" "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{N+1}-\\frac{1}{{N}^{2}+N}=\\frac{1}{N}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362337 :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -1 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,1+\\sqrt{2}\\,$ | $\\,1-\\sqrt{2}\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),$ and its maximum value is $1+\\sqrt{2}.$" "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -1 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $$\n\\displaystyle \\,(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,\n$$ | $$\n\\displaystyle \\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,\n$$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,1+\\sqrt{2}\\,$ | $\\,1-\\sqrt{2}\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),\n$$ and its maximum value is $1+\\sqrt{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186362339 :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,16⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,8y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 16 &=8\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{2}{\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{2}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=4$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+4\\cdot \\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{4+16}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =4 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},\\,y=\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),\\,(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-8\\sqrt{5}\\,$ | $\\,8\\sqrt{5}\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),$ and its minimum value is $-8\\sqrt{5}.$" "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,16⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,8y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 16 &=8\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{2}{\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{2}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=4$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+4\\cdot \\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{4+16}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =4 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},\\,y=\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),\\,(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $$\n\\displaystyle \\,(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})\\,\n$$ | $$\n\\displaystyle \\,(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})\\,\n$$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-8\\sqrt{5}\\,$ | $\\,8\\sqrt{5}\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),\n$$ and its minimum value is $-8\\sqrt{5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186362343 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-z,-y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -z &=2\\lambda y \\\\ -y &=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda -1) &=0 \\\\ y(4{\\lambda }^{2}-1) &=0 \\\\ z &=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne 1$ and $\\lambda \\ne \\pm \\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, the solution of the above system is $x=y=z=0.$ But this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1.$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the second and third equations, we must have $y=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(\\pm 1,0,0).$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, by the first and third equations, we must have $x=0$ and $z=∓y.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+(-y)^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding values of $z$ are\n\n$$\nz=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),(0,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ | $\\,(0,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ | $\\,(\\pm 1,0,0)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\,$ | $\\,∓\\frac{1}{2}\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the points $(\\pm 1,0,0),$  and its maximum value is $1.$" "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-z,-y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -z &=2\\lambda y \\\\ -y &=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda -1) &=0 \\\\ y(4{\\lambda }^{2}-1) &=0 \\\\ z &=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne 1$ and $\\lambda \\ne \\pm \\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, the solution of the above system is $x=y=z=0.$ But this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1.$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the second and third equations, we must have $y=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(\\pm 1,0,0).$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, by the first and third equations, we must have $x=0$ and $z=∓y.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+(-y)^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding values of $z$ are\n\n$$\nz=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $$\n\\displaystyle (0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),(0,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $$\n\\displaystyle \\,(0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,\n$$ | $$\n\\displaystyle \\,(0,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,\n$$ | $\\,(\\pm 1,0,0)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\,$ | $\\,∓\\frac{1}{2}\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the points $(\\pm 1,0,0),$  and its maximum value is $1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186362346 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-1 &=4\\lambda x \\\\ 2 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{1}{4\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=-\\frac{1}{4}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n  Substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4}y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=4,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{32}{9} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{4}y=∓\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}),\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $5+3\\sqrt{2}$ | $5-3\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})\\,,$ and its minimum value is $5-3\\sqrt{2}.$" "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-1 &=4\\lambda x \\\\ 2 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{1}{4\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=-\\frac{1}{4}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n  Substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4}y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=4,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{32}{9} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{4}y=∓\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}),\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})\n$$ | $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})\n$$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $5+3\\sqrt{2}$ | $5-3\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})\\,,\n$$ and its minimum value is $5-3\\sqrt{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186362353 :question/problem "$\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\operatorname{csc}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int ⟨\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\operatorname{csc}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362356 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨2{e}^{t},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨2{e}^{t},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362362 :question/problem "Find $\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},-\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}⟩\\text{d}t.$" "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\int ⟨\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},-\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362363 :question/problem "Find $\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3{e}^{(2t+1)}⟩\\,\\text{d}t.$" "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3{e}^{(2t+1)}⟩\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362389 :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nequals $\\frac{2}{5}.$ Find the value of $k.$" "The entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $\\frac{2}{5}.$ Find the value of $k.$"] [:db/cas 17592186362393 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2t)}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)}{4}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{4})-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-5t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+5t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-4t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4t)}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)}{6}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )}{6})-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “$S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product” is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product”; “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product”; “$S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product”." "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2t)}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)}{4}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{4})-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-5t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+5t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-4t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4t)}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)}{6}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )}{6})-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “$S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product” is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product”; “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product”; “$S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product”."] [:db/cas 17592186362402 :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ k & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nequals $-1.$ Find the value of $k.$" "The entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ k & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $-1.$ Find the value of $k.$"] [:db/cas 17592186362406 :tutorial/content "Let's apply the general algorithm to find the power series solution for the following equation:\n\n$$\ny' - y  =0\n$$\n\n- **Step 1:** First, we write the solution as a general power series $y =\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n}.$\n\n- **Step 2:** Next, we compute $y'$ so that we can substitute the general series solution into the differential equation. Computing $y',$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting in the differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-y & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 3:** Then, we rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value. In the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m = n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 4:** We consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$ Since both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-{a}_{n}{x}^{n}] & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[(n+1){a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n}]{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 5:** Setting the coefficients of the series equal to $0,$ we get the following recurrence relation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n+1){a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n} & =0 \\\\ {a}_{n+1} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{n+1},\\,n=0,1,2…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the following, we'll see how to apply this recurrence relation to find the final power series solution of the differential equation." "Let's apply the general algorithm to find the power series solution for the following equation:\n\n$$\ny' - y  =0\n$$\n\n- **Step 1:** First, we write the solution as a general power series $$\n\\displaystyle y =\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2:** Next, we compute $y'$ so that we can substitute the general series solution into the differential equation. Computing $y',$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting in the differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-y & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 3:** Then, we rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value. In the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m = n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 4:** We consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$ Since both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-{a}_{n}{x}^{n}] & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[(n+1){a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n}]{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 5:** Setting the coefficients of the series equal to $0,$ we get the following recurrence relation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n+1){a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n} & =0 \\\\ {a}_{n+1} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{n+1},\\,n=0,1,2…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the following, we'll see how to apply this recurrence relation to find the final power series solution of the differential equation."] [:db/cas 17592186362496 :question/problem "Water flows over the element $E$ according to the vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=6y\\,i+x\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $E$ found in part 5 has zero $k$-component, it may be considered a two-dimensional vector defined in terms of $i$ and $j$ only. Use this to find an expression for the flux of $F$ across the element in terms of $x$ and $y.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Flux = \n${∮}_{E}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}s.$" "Water flows over the element $E$ according to the vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=6y\\,i+x\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $E$ found in part 5 has zero $k$-component, it may be considered a two-dimensional vector defined in terms of $i$ and $j$ only. Use this to find an expression for the flux of $F$ across the element in terms of $x$ and $y.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Flux = \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{E}\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}s.$"] [:db/cas 17592186362502 :question/problem "![](images/b7/b7d9cf2ef89d1a21cd9062a9ef1e709fb1c98bfc7de75b6b0447a204bd8472fc.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            After some time, the duct's settings are changed. The diagram above shows the flow of air induced at the top of a room in the $xy$-plane around the duct's outlet. The velocity field $F$ in this plane is given by\n\n$$\nF=({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{i}-2xy\\,\\text{j}.\n$$\n\nFind the flux of the airflow across the boundary $C$ of the exit of the tube.\n\n*Hint: First check if $F$ is source-free.*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required flux is $\\,{∮}_{C}^{}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." "![](images/b7/b7d9cf2ef89d1a21cd9062a9ef1e709fb1c98bfc7de75b6b0447a204bd8472fc.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            After some time, the duct's settings are changed. The diagram above shows the flow of air induced at the top of a room in the $xy$-plane around the duct's outlet. The velocity field $F$ in this plane is given by\n\n$$\nF=({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{i}-2xy\\,\\text{j}.\n$$\n\nFind the flux of the airflow across the boundary $C$ of the exit of the tube.\n\n*Hint: First check if $F$ is source-free.*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required flux is $$\n\\displaystyle \\,{∮}_{C}^{}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s\\,=\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ."] [:db/cas 17592186362502 :question/worked-solution "Recall that, if $F=P\\,\\text{i}+Q\\,\\text{j}$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n* $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n* $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n* The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n* Flux is path-independent. That is, given two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and end points, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n* There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, for our vector field $F=({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{i}-2xy\\,\\text{j}$, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2xy) \\\\  & =2x-2x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is a source-free vector field. So, the flux of $F$ across any closed curve is zero. Hence,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$" "Recall that, if $F=P\\,\\text{i}+Q\\,\\text{j}$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n* $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n* $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n* The flux $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s\n$$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n* Flux is path-independent. That is, given two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and end points, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n* There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, for our vector field $F=({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{i}-2xy\\,\\text{j}$, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2xy) \\\\  & =2x-2x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is a source-free vector field. So, the flux of $F$ across any closed curve is zero. Hence,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362505 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to write down a double integral that gives the circulation of $F$ along $C,$ transversed counterclockwise.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Use Green's theorem to write down a double integral that gives the circulation of $F$ along $C,$ transversed counterclockwise.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186362530 :question/problem "${∭}_{R}∇\\cdot G\\,\\text{d}V=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}∇\\cdot G\\,\\text{d}V=\\,\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 175921863625