                 \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{d}A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186362542 :question/problem "Write down the double integral from the previous part in polar coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${∬}_{D}-6xy\\,\\text{d}A\\,={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{a}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{b}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        where $a\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \nand $b\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." "Write down the double integral from the previous part in polar coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}-6xy\\,\\text{d}A\\,={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{a}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{b}\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        where $a\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \nand $b\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ."] [:db/cas 17592186362549 :question/problem "${\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\,=\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ."] [:db/cas 17592186362552 :question/problem "![](images/cf/cfbc570ba4fc6bd4923edc2d0efae0e735e671f9b4350226159e591e0bdae6c7.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The net $L$ is represented by the line segment shown in the diagram above. \nCalculate the flux of $F$ through $L,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{L}^{}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "![](images/cf/cfbc570ba4fc6bd4923edc2d0efae0e735e671f9b4350226159e591e0bdae6c7.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The net $L$ is represented by the line segment shown in the diagram above. \nCalculate the flux of $F$ through $L,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{L}^{}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s\\,=\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186362555 :question/problem "![](images/83/8354a964dc235f1c9676754cf7d163cb4a54f05bd5899ff398acb11a4287861d.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Let $∂R$ be the boundary of the region $R,$ shown above. Use Green's theorem to calculate the following integral:\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${∮}_{∂R}^{}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\,={∮}_{∂R}^{}3x{y}^{2}\\,dx-{y}^{3}\\,dy\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "![](images/83/8354a964dc235f1c9676754cf7d163cb4a54f05bd5899ff398acb11a4287861d.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Let $∂R$ be the boundary of the region $R,$ shown above. Use Green's theorem to calculate the following integral:\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂R}^{}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\,={∮}_{∂R}^{}3x{y}^{2}\\,dx-{y}^{3}\\,dy\\,=\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186362818 :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{1}^{1}{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{1}{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362825 :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{-2}^{-2}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{-2}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362832 :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2,$ calculate\n${\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)+3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2,\n$$ calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)+3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362839 :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=4$ and ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6,$ then what is ${\\int }_{-2}^{3}(f(x)-6)\\,\\text{d}x?$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=4\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6,\n$$ then what is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{3}(f(x)-6)\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362846 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that ${\\int }_{6}^{4}\\frac{1}{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-2$ and ${\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-8.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{6}^{4}\\frac{1}{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-2\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-8.\n$$ What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362853 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that ${\\int }_{5}^{3}4f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1$ and ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{5}^{3}4f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2.\n$$ What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362860 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int (2x+1){e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2x+1){e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362860 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int (2x+1){e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x+1\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{3x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (2x+1)\\cdot {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =(2x+1)\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}-\\int \\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2x+1){e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{(2x+1){e}^{3x}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x+1){e}^{3x}}{3}-\\frac{2{e}^{3x}}{9}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{(6x+1){e}^{3x}}{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int (2x+1){e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x+1\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{3x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (2x+1)\\cdot {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =(2x+1)\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}-\\int \\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2x+1){e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{(2x+1){e}^{3x}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x+1){e}^{3x}}{3}-\\frac{2{e}^{3x}}{9}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{(6x+1){e}^{3x}}{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362867 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1/4}x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/4}x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362887 :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=0,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ then ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=2.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(1)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & ≈L(1) \\\\  & =2(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=0,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=2.\n$$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(1)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & ≈L(1) \\\\  & =2(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362894 :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3}{3x-4},$ then ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{3}{3(2)-4}=\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{3}{2}(x-2).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(3)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & ≈L(3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{3}{2}(3-2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3}{3x-4},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{3}{3(2)-4}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{3}{2}(x-2).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(3)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & ≈L(3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{3}{2}(3-2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362996 :question/problem "$\\int 3x{e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 3x{e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186362996 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int 3x{e}^{2x+5}\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{2x+5}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 3x\\cdot {e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =3x\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3x{e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3x{e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\int {e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3x{e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\frac{3{e}^{2x+5}}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 3x{e}^{2x+5}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{2x+5}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 3x\\cdot {e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =3x\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3x{e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3x{e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\int {e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3x{e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\frac{3{e}^{2x+5}}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186363003 :question/problem "Calculate  $\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186363003 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe, substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}\\right)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)}{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe, substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}\\right)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)}{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186363038 :question/problem "Given that $D={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+3x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\frac{1}{x}?$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+3x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\frac{1}{x}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186363059 :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{169-{y}^{2}}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨6,5⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6,5)-f(0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨6,5⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\ \\sqrt{169-{5}^{2}}-\\sqrt{169-{0}^{2}} & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨6,5⟩ \\\\ 12-13 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨6,5⟩ \\\\ -1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨6,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨0,-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{169-{y}^{2}}}⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨0,\\,-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{\\sqrt{169-{{c}_{2}}^{2}}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨6,5⟩ \\\\ -1 & =⟨0,\\,-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{\\sqrt{169-{{c}_{2}}^{2}}}⟩\\cdot ⟨6,5⟩ \\\\ -1 & =-\\frac{5{c}_{2}}{\\sqrt{169-{{c}_{2}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\sqrt{169-{{c}_{2}}^{2}} & =5{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve this radical equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{169-{{c}_{2}}^{2}} & =5{c}_{2} \\\\ 169-{{c}_{2}}^{2} & =25{{c}_{2}}^{2} \\\\ 26{{c}_{2}}^{2} & =169 \\\\ {{c}_{2}}^{2} & =\\frac{169}{26} \\\\ {{c}_{2}}^{2} & =\\frac{13}{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the radical equation, we notice that ${c}_{2}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2}$ does not satisfy the equation. Therefore, the only solution is ${c}_{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2}.$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}$ to find ${c}_{1}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-0}{5-0} & =\\frac{x-0}{6-0} \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{6}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{6}{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking into account that ${c}_{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2},$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2} & =\\frac{5}{6}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{26}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{26}}{5},\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2}).$" "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{169-{y}^{2}}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨6,5⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6,5)-f(0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨6,5⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\ \\sqrt{169-{5}^{2}}-\\sqrt{169-{0}^{2}} & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨6,5⟩ \\\\ 12-13 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨6,5⟩ \\\\ -1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨6,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨0,-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{169-{y}^{2}}}⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨0,\\,-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{\\sqrt{169-{{c}_{2}}^{2}}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨6,5⟩ \\\\ -1 & =⟨0,\\,-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{\\sqrt{169-{{c}_{2}}^{2}}}⟩\\cdot ⟨6,5⟩ \\\\ -1 & =-\\frac{5{c}_{2}}{\\sqrt{169-{{c}_{2}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\sqrt{169-{{c}_{2}}^{2}} & =5{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve this radical equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{169-{{c}_{2}}^{2}} & =5{c}_{2} \\\\ 169-{{c}_{2}}^{2} & =25{{c}_{2}}^{2} \\\\ 26{{c}_{2}}^{2} & =169 \\\\ {{c}_{2}}^{2} & =\\frac{169}{26} \\\\ {{c}_{2}}^{2} & =\\frac{13}{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the radical equation, we notice that ${c}_{2}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2}$ does not satisfy the equation. Therefore, the only solution is ${c}_{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2}.$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}$ to find ${c}_{1}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-0}{5-0} & =\\frac{x-0}{6-0} \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{6}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{6}{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking into account that ${c}_{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2},$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2} & =\\frac{5}{6}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{26}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{3\\sqrt{26}}{5},\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{2}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186363238 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{6}$ into ${R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=6 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=6$ is false. Since ${{v}_{1}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T),$ we must have that $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$ and by the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ is not invertible. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $T$ is not invertible, $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 6.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is a bijection” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 6,$ the transformation $T$ is not a bijection.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$ is true. Since ${{v}_{1}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T),$ we must have that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim(Ker}(T))=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$ only." "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{6}$ into ${R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=6 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=6$ is false. Since ${{v}_{1}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T),$ we must have that $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$ and by the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ is not invertible. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $T$ is not invertible, $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 6.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is a bijection” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 6,$ the transformation $T$ is not a bijection.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$ is true. Since ${{v}_{1}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T),$ we must have that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim(Ker}(T))=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186363363 :question/problem "![](images/03/03cacb8c2da76ff806a3f8a41cf18d3b57f67eeb60d6efa0cdfbfce770496c60.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={e}^{-\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ shown above?" "![](images/03/03cacb8c2da76ff806a3f8a41cf18d3b57f67eeb60d6efa0cdfbfce770496c60.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={e}^{-\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ shown above?"] [:db/cas 17592186363370 :question/problem "![](images/c6/c6556825089b3b475615ed4a8ebf9eff2e6db3d76eb26103312f736a3e114793.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8},$ shown above?" "![](images/c6/c6556825089b3b475615ed4a8ebf9eff2e6db3d76eb26103312f736a3e114793.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8},$ shown above?"] [:db/cas 17592186363377 :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta $ for $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186363405 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{0}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is true. Since statements “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” and ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$”; ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$; “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function”." "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{0}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is true. Since statements “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$”; $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1;\n$$ “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function”."] [:db/cas 17592186363465 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x+1,2y+1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global maximum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=3\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x+1,2y+1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =1$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 1,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y+1 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{2(\\lambda -1)} \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2(\\lambda -1)}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ in the condition $g(x,y)=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}),\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2})$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $3-\\sqrt{6}$ | $3+\\sqrt{6}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}),$ and its maximum value is $3+\\sqrt{6}.$" "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x+1,2y+1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global maximum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=3\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x+1,2y+1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =1$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 1,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y+1 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{2(\\lambda -1)} \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2(\\lambda -1)}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ in the condition $g(x,y)=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}),\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$ | $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2})\n$$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $3-\\sqrt{6}$ | $3+\\sqrt{6}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}),\n$$ and its maximum value is $3+\\sqrt{6}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186363520 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =4, & \\,{m}_{2} & =6, & \\,{m}_{3} & =8, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-3, & \\,{x}_{2} & =-1, & \\,{x}_{3} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =4+6+8 \\\\  & =18\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-3)+6\\cdot (-1)+8\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-12-6+24 \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{6}{18}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =4, & \\,{m}_{2} & =6, & \\,{m}_{3} & =8, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-3, & \\,{x}_{2} & =-1, & \\,{x}_{3} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =4+6+8 \\\\  & =18\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-3)+6\\cdot (-1)+8\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-12-6+24 \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{6}{18}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186363534 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186363541 :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(0,-1)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+2(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡t+3{sin}^{2}⁡t+2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =3+2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (3+2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives two solutions $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain three candidate points:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt{3},0),\\,(0,-\\sqrt{3}),\\,(0,\\sqrt{3})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{3},0) & =(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{0}^{2}+2(0) \\\\  & =3+0+0 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ f(0,-\\sqrt{3}) & =(0)^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+2(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =0+3-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =3-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ f(0,\\sqrt{3}) & ={0}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+2(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =0+3+2\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =3+2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ f(0,-1) & ={0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+2(-1) \\\\  & =0+1-2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(0,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $3+2\\sqrt{3}$ at the point $(0,\\sqrt{3}).$" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(0,-1)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+2(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡t+3{sin}^{2}⁡t+2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =3+2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (3+2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives two solutions $$\n\\displaystyle t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain three candidate points:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt{3},0),\\,(0,-\\sqrt{3}),\\,(0,\\sqrt{3})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{3},0) & =(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{0}^{2}+2(0) \\\\  & =3+0+0 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ f(0,-\\sqrt{3}) & =(0)^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+2(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =0+3-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =3-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ f(0,\\sqrt{3}) & ={0}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+2(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =0+3+2\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =3+2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ f(0,-1) & ={0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+2(-1) \\\\  & =0+1-2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(0,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $3+2\\sqrt{3}$ at the point $(0,\\sqrt{3}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186364047 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of the form\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIf $f≡0$ and $g≡0$ (i.e., both $f$ and $g$ are zero functions), then the system is *homogeneous*. Otherwise, if at least one of $f$ or $g$ is not identically zero, the system is *inhomogeneous*.\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=3, &  & b(t)=2, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=4, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-2{x}_{1}(t)+5\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-1, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=-2, &  & d(t)=0, &  & g(t)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=7{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=0, &  & b(t)=0, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=7, &  & d(t)=3, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$; $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=7{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\en