nce $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$\n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}$ is false. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)>0$ is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$ In turn, this means that $\\text{nullity}(T)>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{nullity}(T)>0$ only." "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\}$ is false. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)>0$ is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$ In turn, this means that $\\text{nullity}(T)>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{nullity}(T)>0$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186364213 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$ is true. Since the columns of $T$ satisfies ${v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=0$, then they are linearly dependent, which gives us $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Therefore, according to the Invertible Matrix Theorem, we must have $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=3$ is false. Since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is a bijection” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3},$ the transformation $T$ can't be a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$ only." "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$ is true. Since the columns of $T$ satisfies ${v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=0$, then they are linearly dependent, which gives us $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Therefore, according to the Invertible Matrix Theorem, we must have $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=3$ is false. Since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is a bijection” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3},$ the transformation $T$ can't be a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186364268 :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2$, calculate\n${\\int }_{4}^{0}(2x-6f(x))\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2,\n$$ calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{4}^{0}(2x-6f(x))\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186364275 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }12x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }12x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186364302 :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,1,0)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$r=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,1,0)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$$\n\\displaystyle r=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186364323 :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{1/2}^{1/2}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1/2}^{1/2}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186364330 :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2$ then what is ${\\int }_{-2}^{1}(5+4f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2\n$$ then what is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{1}(5+4f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186364392 :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=9$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(9)+{f}^{′}(9)(x-9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(9)=-10,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}},$ then ${f}^{′}(9)=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{9}}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=-10-\\frac{2}{3}(x-9).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(8.7)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=8.7.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(8.7) & ≈L(8.7) \\\\  & =-10-\\frac{2}{3}(8.7-9) \\\\  & =-10-\\frac{2}{3}(-0.3) \\\\  & =-10+0.2 \\\\  & =-9.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=9$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(9)+{f}^{′}(9)(x-9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(9)=-10,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(9)=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{9}}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=-10-\\frac{2}{3}(x-9).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(8.7)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=8.7.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(8.7) & ≈L(8.7) \\\\  & =-10-\\frac{2}{3}(8.7-9) \\\\  & =-10-\\frac{2}{3}(-0.3) \\\\  & =-10+0.2 \\\\  & =-9.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186364406 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le 0.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le 0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186364620 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\left(-\\frac{6}{18}\\right)\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}=\\left(\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\cdot (3\\sqrt{2})=4.$" "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\left(-\\frac{6}{18}\\right)\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle {q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}=\\left(\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\cdot (3\\sqrt{2})=4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186364627 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}=\\left(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right)\\cdot (\\sqrt{6})=-2.$" "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle {q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}=\\left(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right)\\cdot (\\sqrt{6})=-2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186364763 :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ={x}_{1}(1)=1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(1) & ={x}_{2}(1)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} &={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} &={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-\\frac{5}{12}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{6}y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-\\frac{5}{12}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} &={x}_{2},\\, && {x}_{1}(1)=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} &=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{1}-\\frac{5}{12}{x}_{2},\\, && {x}_{2}(1)=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=1,q=-2,f(t)=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{1}-\\frac{5}{12}{x}_{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-q)\\cdot f(t) & =(1-(-2))(\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{1}-\\frac{5}{12}{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{1}-\\frac{5}{12}{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{1}-\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ={x}_{1}(1)=1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(1) & ={x}_{2}(1)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} &={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} &={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-\\frac{5}{12}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{6}y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-\\frac{5}{12}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} &={x}_{2},\\, && {x}_{1}(1)=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} &=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{1}-\\frac{5}{12}{x}_{2},\\, && {x}_{2}(1)=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle p=1,q=-2,f(t)=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{1}-\\frac{5}{12}{x}_{2},\n$$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-q)\\cdot f(t) & =(1-(-2))(\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{1}-\\frac{5}{12}{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{1}-\\frac{5}{12}{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{1}-\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186364852 :question/problem "The domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{4x+3}{5x+2k}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\frac{2}{5})\\cup (\\frac{2}{5},\\infty ).$ What is the value of $k?$" "The domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{4x+3}{5x+2k}$ is $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\infty ,\\frac{2}{5})\\cup (\\frac{2}{5},\\infty ).\n$$ What is the value of $k?$"] [:db/cas 17592186364866 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{cot}^{4}⁡x-1}{{cot}^{2}⁡x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{cot}^{4}⁡x-1}{{cot}^{2}⁡x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186364887 :question/worked-solution "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 15-3t & =0 \\\\ t & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(15-3t) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(5)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (15-3t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =15t-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=14.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15(1)-\\frac{3}{2}(1)^{2}+C & =14 \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{2}-1\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{2}+15t-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(5)=\\frac{1}{2}+15(5)-\\frac{3}{2}(5)^{2}=38\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$" "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 15-3t & =0 \\\\ t & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(15-3t) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(5)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (15-3t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =15t-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=14.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15(1)-\\frac{3}{2}(1)^{2}+C & =14 \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{2}-1\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $$\n\\displaystyle v(t)=\\frac{1}{2}+15t-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(5)=\\frac{1}{2}+15(5)-\\frac{3}{2}(5)^{2}=38\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186364941 :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\,p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\right)\\cdot p(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\,p(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\,{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{2(0)}{{({0}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\,p(0)+\\frac{1}{{0}^{2}+1}\\,{p}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =0(2\\,i+j-k)+(\\,i-2\\,j-2k) \\\\  & =\\,i-2\\,j-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(0):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖r(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(0):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(0)}{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (i-2\\,j-2\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,i-\\frac{2}{3}\\,j-\\frac{2}{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=0$ is  $T(0)=\\frac{1}{3}\\,i-\\frac{2}{3}\\,j-\\frac{2}{3}\\,k.$" "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\,p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\right)\\cdot p(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\,p(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\,{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{2(0)}{{({0}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\,p(0)+\\frac{1}{{0}^{2}+1}\\,{p}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =0(2\\,i+j-k)+(\\,i-2\\,j-2k) \\\\  & =\\,i-2\\,j-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(0):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖r(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(0):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(0)}{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (i-2\\,j-2\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,i-\\frac{2}{3}\\,j-\\frac{2}{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=0$ is  $$\n\\displaystyle T(0)=\\frac{1}{3}\\,i-\\frac{2}{3}\\,j-\\frac{2}{3}\\,k.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186364948 :question/problem "Find the value of $|\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|.$" "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186364968 :question/problem "Given the linear transformation\n $T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3y-x-4z \\\\ 2x+7z-6y \\\\ 16z+4x-12y\\end{bmatrix},$ find $\\text{nullity}(T).$" "Given the linear transformation\n $$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3y-x-4z \\\\ 2x+7z-6y \\\\ 16z+4x-12y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $\\text{nullity}(T).$"] [:db/cas 17592186365115 :question/problem "${\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}({i}^{2}+{j}^{2})=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}({i}^{2}+{j}^{2})=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186365185 :question/problem "![](images/22/22058df7ac75e085e1dc352736d1abf289e80d68f340ee63687e69d18e9b23c2.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\pi -x}\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz.$ The region of integration is shown above.\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.$*" "![](images/22/22058df7ac75e085e1dc352736d1abf289e80d68f340ee63687e69d18e9b23c2.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\pi -x}\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz.\n$$ The region of integration is shown above.\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186365206 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{z/x}^{x}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$*" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{z/x}^{x}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186365226 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-y=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2x,-2y,2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,-1,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y &=-\\lambda \\\\ 2z &=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(1+\\lambda )x &=0 \\\\ y &=\\frac{\\lambda }{2} \\\\ (1-\\lambda )z &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne \\pm 1.$ Then, by the first and third equations, we must have $x=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\ny={0}^{2}+{0}^{2}\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,0,0).$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =-1.$ Then, by the second and third equations, we must have $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $z=0,$ but this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0.$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the first and second equations, we must have $x=0$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}={0}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\,⇒\\,z=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,0,0)\\,$ | $\\,(0,\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the points $(0,\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{1}{4}.$" "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-y=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2x,-2y,2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,-1,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y &=-\\lambda \\\\ 2z &=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(1+\\lambda )x &=0 \\\\ y &=\\frac{\\lambda }{2} \\\\ (1-\\lambda )z &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne \\pm 1.$ Then, by the first and third equations, we must have $x=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\ny={0}^{2}+{0}^{2}\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,0,0).$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =-1.$ Then, by the second and third equations, we must have $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $z=0,$ but this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0.$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the first and second equations, we must have $x=0$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}={0}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\,⇒\\,z=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,0,0)\\,$ | $$\n\\displaystyle \\,(0,\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,\n$$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,0\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the points $(0,\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{1}{4}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186365233 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =2, & \\,{m}_{2} & =4, & \\,{m}_{3} & =1, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2, & \\,{x}_{2} & =-1.5, & \\,{x}_{3} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =2+4+1 \\\\  & =7\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-1.5)+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4-6+3 \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$" "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =2, & \\,{m}_{2} & =4, & \\,{m}_{3} & =1, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2, & \\,{x}_{2} & =-1.5, & \\,{x}_{3} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =2+4+1 \\\\  & =7\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-1.5)+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4-6+3 \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186365571 :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{2}^{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1)\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $m$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}]$" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $m$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+1)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\n$$\n{{field-1}}$)\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}]$"] [:db/cas 17592186365579 :question/problem "The center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{49}-\\frac{(x+8)^{2}}{36}=1$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" "The center of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{49}-\\frac{(x+8)^{2}}{36}=1\n$$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$"] [:db/cas 17592186365584 :question/problem "The center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+7)^{2}}{100}-\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{64}=1$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" "The center of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(y+7)^{2}}{100}-\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{64}=1\n$$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$"] [:db/cas 17592186365640 :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z)\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-4y{y}^{′} & =6x \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =6x+4y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{6x+4y{y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{6x+4yz}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=0.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(1)=0, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{6x+4yz}{{x}^{2}},\\, && z(1)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=\\frac{5}{4},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5}{4}-1=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{6x+4yz}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |  | $0$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $0$ |  |  | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)≈0$\nand\n${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)=z\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)≈\\frac{3}{2}.$" "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z)\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-4y{y}^{′} & =6x \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =6x+4y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{6x+4y{y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in th