ned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $0$ |  |  | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)≈0$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle {y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)=z\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)≈\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186365797 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186365800 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ 7t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ |  | $8t-14$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  |  |" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ 7t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{4}\n$$ |  | $8t-14$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186365808 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}4t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | $-2t-3$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  |  |" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}4t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\,\\,\n$$ |  | $-2t-3$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186365845 :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=0$ and $y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+{y}^{′}\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-20\\lambda m+100{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-10\\lambda )(m-10\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=10\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{10\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{10\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{10\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA+B\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=Bx{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=B{e}^{10\\lambda x}+10B\\lambda x{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+{y}^{′}\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}B{e}^{5\\lambda }+B{e}^{5\\lambda }+5B\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}B{e}^{5\\lambda }+5B\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{5\\lambda }+\\frac{10}{3}B\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{5\\lambda }(1+\\frac{10}{3}\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require $B\\ne 0.$ Thus, since ${e}^{5\\lambda }>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding eigenfunction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =Bx{e}^{10\\lambda x} \\\\  & =Bx{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=0$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+{y}^{′}\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=0\n$$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-20\\lambda m+100{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-10\\lambda )(m-10\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=10\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{10\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{10\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{10\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA+B\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=Bx{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=B{e}^{10\\lambda x}+10B\\lambda x{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $$\n\\displaystyle y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+{y}^{′}\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=0\n$$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}B{e}^{5\\lambda }+B{e}^{5\\lambda }+5B\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}B{e}^{5\\lambda }+5B\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{5\\lambda }+\\frac{10}{3}B\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{5\\lambda }(1+\\frac{10}{3}\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require $B\\ne 0.$ Thus, since ${e}^{5\\lambda }>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding eigenfunction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =Bx{e}^{10\\lambda x} \\\\  & =Bx{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186365858 :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-10\\lambda m+25{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-5\\lambda )(m-5\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=5\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{5\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{5\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{5\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5A\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda x}+B{e}^{5\\lambda x}+5B\\lambda x{e}^{5\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5A\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda }+B{e}^{5\\lambda }+5B\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 5A\\lambda +B+5B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{10\\lambda }+2B{e}^{10\\lambda }+2(5A\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda }+B{e}^{10\\lambda }+10B\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(5A\\lambda +B+10B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+10A\\lambda +2B+20B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )A+(4+20\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )A+4(1+5\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B &=0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )A+4(1+5\\lambda )B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}5\\lambda  & 1+5\\lambda  \\\\ 1+10\\lambda  & 4(1+5\\lambda )\\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+5\\lambda )-(1+5\\lambda )(1+10\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+5\\lambda )[20\\lambda -(1+10\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+5\\lambda )[20\\lambda -1-10\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+5\\lambda )(10\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{10}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+(1-1)B=0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A=0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{10},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{3}{2}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $Bx{e}^{-x}\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-3).$" "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-10\\lambda m+25{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-5\\lambda )(m-5\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=5\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{5\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{5\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{5\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5A\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambd