unction. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(3)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))$" "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=f(x),\\,y(3)=-2\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $4$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(3)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$   \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi n}{2}x))\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186365989 :question/worked-solution "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 7-7{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 7(1-t)(1+t) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t⩾0$, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7-7{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =-14t \\\\ {v}^{″}(1) & =-14(1) \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(1)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (7-7{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =7t-\\frac{7}{3}{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(2)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(2)-\\frac{7}{3}(2)^{3}+C & =1 \\\\ 14-\\frac{56}{3}+C & =1\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{17}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{17}{3}+7t-\\frac{7}{3}{t}^{3}.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(1)=\\frac{17}{3}+7(1)-\\frac{7}{3}(1)^{3}=\\frac{31}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{31}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached at $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$" "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 7-7{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 7(1-t)(1+t) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t⩾0$, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7-7{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =-14t \\\\ {v}^{″}(1) & =-14(1) \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(1)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (7-7{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =7t-\\frac{7}{3}{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(2)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(2)-\\frac{7}{3}(2)^{3}+C & =1 \\\\ 14-\\frac{56}{3}+C & =1\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{17}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $$\n\\displaystyle v(t)=\\frac{17}{3}+7t-\\frac{7}{3}{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(1)=\\frac{17}{3}+7(1)-\\frac{7}{3}(1)^{3}=\\frac{31}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{31}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached at $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186366013 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$C=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 4 & 5 & -12 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 4 & 5 & -12 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366039 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{rank}(T)=2,$ then $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$” is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement “If $[-2,0,0]^{T}$ is a solution of $T(x)=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$” is true. Since $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is a solution of $Tx=0$, we have that $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and therefore $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement “If $T(x)=[1,0,0]^{T}$ has no solution then $\\text{rank}(T)=3$” is false. Since $T(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has no solution, we have that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Hence, $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct option is “If $[-2,0,0]^{T}$ is a solution of $T(x)=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$” only." "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{rank}(T)=2,$ then $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$” is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement “If $[-2,0,0]^{T}$ is a solution of $T(x)=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$” is true. Since $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is a solution of $Tx=0$, we have that $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and therefore $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement “If $T(x)=[1,0,0]^{T}$ has no solution then $\\text{rank}(T)=3$” is false. Since $T(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has no solution, we have that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Hence, $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct option is “If $[-2,0,0]^{T}$ is a solution of $T(x)=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186366097 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{n}$ into ${R}^{n},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{Ker}(T)={⟨x,y,z⟩\\,|\\,x+y=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)+1=n$” is false. Solving $x+y=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-y \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=y\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}+z\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(T)+2 & =n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If there exists a vector $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ then $|T|\\ne 0$” is false. If $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T)$ then $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{n}$, so, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ is not a bijection. Thus, the matrix $T$ is not invertible, and, consequently, $|T|=0.$\n\n- Statement “If ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent and $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$” is false. Since $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=0$, then ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$ But the vectors are linearly independent, therefore, the dimension of  $\\text{Ker}(T)$ is at least $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{n}$ into ${R}^{n},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{Ker}(T)={⟨x,y,z⟩\\,|\\,x+y=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)+1=n$” is false. Solving $x+y=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-y \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=y\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}+z\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(T)+2 & =n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If there exists a vector $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ then $|T|\\ne 0$” is false. If $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T)$ then $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{n}$, so, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ is not a bijection. Thus, the matrix $T$ is not invertible, and, consequently, $|T|=0.$\n\n- Statement “If ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent and $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$” is false. Since $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=0$, then ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$ But the vectors are linearly independent, therefore, the dimension of  $\\text{Ker}(T)$ is at least $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""] [:db/cas 17592186366104 :question/problem "![](images/48/48d76f34becfba9f58658c3c38ba2df0e76ad6ff20b83ffc836e213c4eea4565.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[6]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+{z}^{2})dz\\,dy\\,dx.$ The region of integration is shown above.\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*" "![](images/48/48d76f34becfba9f58658c3c38ba2df0e76ad6ff20b83ffc836e213c4eea4565.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[6]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+{z}^{2})dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$ The region of integration is shown above.\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186366118 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 2}{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis.\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthonormal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\frac{1}{3},$ $b=\\frac{2}{3},$ $c=\\frac{2}{3}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|-\\frac{1}{3}|+|\\frac{2}{3}|+|\\frac{2}{3}|=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$" "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 2}{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis.\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is an orthonormal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\frac{1}{3},$ $b=\\frac{2}{3},$ $c=\\frac{2}{3}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|-\\frac{1}{3}|+|\\frac{2}{3}|+|\\frac{2}{3}|=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366125 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=4,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{0}{4}=0.\n$$" "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=4,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{0}{4}=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366132 :question/worked-solution "We find an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{8}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the vector ${v}_{3}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{6}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{18}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-8\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 6 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\frac{3}{2},$ $c=\\frac{3}{2},$ and $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}=-1.$" "We find an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{8}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the vector ${v}_{3}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{6}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{18}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-8\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 6 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\frac{3}{2},$ $c=\\frac{3}{2},$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}=-1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366158 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{4}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(4+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].$" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{4}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(4+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366240 :question/problem "${\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}(2i+j)=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}(2i+j)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366247 :question/problem "${\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{2}^{i}{3}^{j}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{2}^{i}{3}^{j}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366254 :question/problem "${\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}(i-j)=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}(i-j)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366316 :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1?$" "What is the center of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366323 :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{2}=1?$" "What is the center of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{2}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366379 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{5\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 3}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=6,$ $b=\\frac{9}{2},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{(6)}{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$" "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{5\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 3}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=6,$ $b=\\frac{9}{2},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{(6)}{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366386 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{-2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11},$ $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11},$ $c=\\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11}|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11}|+|\\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}|=\\frac{5\\sqrt{11}}{11}.\n$$" "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{-2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is an orthonormal basis of $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11},$ $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11},$ $c=\\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11}|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11}|+|\\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}|=\\frac{5\\sqrt{11}}{11}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366427 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ is true. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is a bijection” is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$; $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ only." "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a 