 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $\\begin{gathered}\\text{Ker}(T)=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}\\,[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\\\\\,y-2z=0,\\\\x+z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$” is true. Solving $y-2z=0$ for $y,$ we get $y=2z.$ Solving $x+z=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-z.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-z \\\\ 2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=z\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$” is true. If the columns of $T$ are such that ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2},$ then the set of vectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent and therefore $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Now, according to the matrix invertible theorem, we obtain that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $\\text{Im}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,x=0}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$”; “If $\\begin{gathered}\\text{Ker}(T)=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}\\,[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\\\\\,y-2z=0,\\\\x+z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$”; “If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$”." "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{Im}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,x=0}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$” is true. Any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=y\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}+z\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\text{Ker}(T)=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}\\,[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\\\\\,y-2z=0,\\\\x+z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$” is true. Solving $y-2z=0$ for $y,$ we get $y=2z.$ Solving $x+z=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-z.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-z \\\\ 2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=z\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$” is true. If the columns of $T$ are such that ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2},$ then the set of vectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent and therefore $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Now, according to the matrix invertible theorem, we obtain that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $\\text{Im}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,x=0}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$”; “If $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\text{Ker}(T)=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}\\,[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\\\\\,y-2z=0,\\\\x+z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$”; “If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$”."] [:db/cas 17592186366518 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366525 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{5}{3},$ $b=\\frac{1}{3},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}=5.\n$$" "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{5}{3},$ $b=\\frac{1}{3},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}=5.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366697 :question/worked-solution "We find an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{6}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the vector ${v}_{3}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-1,$ $c=2,$ and $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{(-1)}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" "We find an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{6}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the vector ${v}_{3}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-1,$ $c=2,$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{(-1)}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186366704 :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is \n\n$$\n{C}_{1}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{9}=0.\n$$\n\nThis equation defines the single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane. \n\nNotice that the other equations do not define a single point: \n\n- ${C}_{2}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines a pair of intersecting lines in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- ${C}_{3}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{4}=1$ is the equation of a hyperbola with semiaxes $3$ and $2.$\n\n- ${C}_{4}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}=2{x}_{1}^{2}$ is the equation of a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${x}_{2}$-axis.\n\n- ${C}_{5}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{9}=-1$ has no points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane that satisfy the equation (imaginary ellipse)." "The correct answer is \n\n$$\n{C}_{1}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{9}=0.\n$$\n\nThis equation defines the single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane. \n\nNotice that the other equations do not define a single point: \n\n- ${C}_{2}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines a pair of intersecting lines in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {C}_{3}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{4}=1\n$$ is the equation of a hyperbola with semiaxes $3$ and $2.$\n\n- ${C}_{4}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}=2{x}_{1}^{2}$ is the equation of a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${x}_{2}$-axis.\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {C}_{5}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{9}=-1\n$$ has no points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane that satisfy the equation (imaginary ellipse)."] [:db/cas 17592186366711 :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is \n\n$$\n{C}_{4}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=1\n$$\n\n is the equation of a ellipse with semiaxes $1$ and $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nNotice that the other equations do not define an ellipse:\n\n- ${C}_{1}\\,:\\,2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines the single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- ${C}_{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{9}=1$ is the equation of a hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $3.$\n\n- ${C}_{3}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}^{2}=4{x}_{1}$ is the equation of a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${x}_{1}$-axis.\n\n- ${C}_{5}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ is the equation two intersecting lines in the  in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane." "The correct answer is \n\n$$\n{C}_{4}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=1\n$$\n\n is the equation of a ellipse with semiaxes $1$ and $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nNotice that the other equations do not define an ellipse:\n\n- ${C}_{1}\\,:\\,2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines the single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {C}_{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{9}=1\n$$ is the equation of a hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $3.$\n\n- ${C}_{3}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}^{2}=4{x}_{1}$ is the equation of a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${x}_{1}$-axis.\n\n- ${C}_{5}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ is the equation two intersecting lines in the  in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane."] [:db/cas 17592186366752 :question/problem "Let\n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" "Let\n$$\n\\displaystyle T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto"] [:db/cas 17592186366759 :question/problem "Let\n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" "Let\n$$\n\\displaystyle T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto"] [:db/cas 17592186366766 :question/problem "Let\n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" "Let\n$$\n\\displaystyle T=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto"] [:db/cas 17592186367163 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int (2x-{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2x-{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367261 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{t\\to \\pi }f(t),$ if $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\pi }{t} \\\\ \\frac{2t}{\\pi } \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi -t)}{\\pi -t}\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Calculate ${lim}_{t\\to \\pi }f(t),$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\pi }{t} \\\\ \\frac{2t}{\\pi } \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi -t)}{\\pi -t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367268 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{t\\to \\pi }‖f(t)‖,$ if $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\pi }{t} \\\\ \\frac{2t}{\\pi } \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi -t)}{\\pi -t}\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Calculate ${lim}_{t\\to \\pi }‖f(t)‖,$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\pi }{t} \\\\ \\frac{2t}{\\pi } \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi -t)}{\\pi -t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367324 :question/problem "Find the cofactor of ${q}_{55}$ in \n$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 6 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 15 & -1 & -5 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 3 & 8 & 3 \\\\ 18 & 3 & 0 & -9 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the cofactor of ${q}_{55}$ in \n$$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 6 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 15 & -1 & -5 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 3 & 8 & 3 \\\\ 18 & 3 & 0 & -9 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367401 :question/problem "Find \n$|\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3q & -6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & p\\end{aligned}|$\ngiven that \n$|\\begin{aligned}3q & -6 \\\\ 2 & p\\end{aligned}|=-5.$" "Find \n$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3q & -6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & p\\end{aligned}|\n$$\ngiven that \n$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}3q & -6 \\\\ 2 & p\\end{aligned}|=-5.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367408 :question/problem "Find $\\,|\\begin{aligned}p & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & q & 5 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ r & 0 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}|$ given that $\\,|\\begin{aligned}p & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & q & 5 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|=12.$" "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\,|\\begin{aligned}p & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & q & 5 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ r & 0 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}|\n$$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\,|\\begin{aligned}p & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & q & 5 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|=12.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367603 :question/problem "$\\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367603 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367631 :question/problem "Consider the curve $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-{y}^{3}=-8.$ Given that $x=x(t),$   $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{12}$ on the curve at the point where $x=1,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." "Consider the curve $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-{y}^{3}=-8.$ Given that $x=x(t),$   $y=y(t),$  and that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{12}\n$$ on the curve at the point where $x=1,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point."] [:db/cas 17592186367631 :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-{y}^{3} & =-8 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-8) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}))-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{x}\\right)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (3{y}^{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{12}$ and $x=1.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 3-\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=1$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)-{y}^{3} & =-8 \\\\ 0-{y}^{3} & =-8 \\\\ {y}^{3} & =8 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 3-\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-{y}^{3} & =-8 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-8) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}))-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{x}\\right)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (3{y}^{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{12}\n$$ and $x=1.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 3-\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=1$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)-{y}^{3} & =-8 \\\\ 0-{y}^{3} & =-8 \\\\ {y}^{3} & =8 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 3-\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367694 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/9c/9c4ea0ba92e19a201e3944defa6deb0bc1ee76971adaa809521722b6ac31241f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{9-({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}=\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}{(\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}(9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{9}{2}r-\\frac{1}{6}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )]}_{r=0}^{r=3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{9}{2}(3)-\\frac{1}{6}(3)^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{27}{2}-\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[\\frac{27}{2}\\theta -\\frac{9}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]}_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{9}{4}(0-0) \\\\  & =27\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/9c/9c4ea0ba92e19a201e3944defa6deb0bc1ee76971adaa809521722b6ac31241f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $$\n\\displaystyle z=\\sqrt{9-({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}=\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}}z\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}{(\\sqrt{9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}(9-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{9}{2}r-\\frac{1}{6}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )]}_{r=0}^{r=3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{9}{2}(3)-\\frac{1}{6}(3)^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{27}{2}-\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[\\frac{27}{2}\\theta -\\frac{9}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]}_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{9}{4}(0-0) \\\\  & =27\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367722 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\,-8\\le z\\le x+2\\},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}V=$" "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\,-8\\le z\\le x+2\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367729 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\,y\\le 0,\\,y\\le z\\le 6},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,\\text{d}V=$" "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\,y\\le 0,\\,y\\le z\\le 6},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367942 :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y &=3 \\\\ x-2y &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-7)}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y &=3 \\\\ x-2y &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-7)}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367949 :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-16x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{2}-16x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(16x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(16x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{4},$ $x=1,$ and $y=4,$ and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 16 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2\\cdot 4)-\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot 16 & =0 \\\\ 8\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-4 & =0 \\\\ 8\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-16x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{2}-16x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(16x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(16x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$ $x=1,$ and $y=4,$ and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 16 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2\\cdot 4)-\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot 16 & =0 \\\\ 8\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-4 & =0 \\\\ 8\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367971 :question/problem "Find \n$|\\begin{aligned}-3 & b & 0 \\\\ d & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}|$\ngiven that \n$|\\begin{aligned}-3 & b \\\\ d & 2\\end{aligned}|=-7.$" "Find \n$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}-3 & b & 0 \\\\ d & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}|\n$$\ngiven that \n$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}-3 & b \\\\ d & 2\\end{aligned}|=-7.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186367992 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)$, if $f(s)=⟨4{s}^{7}+13,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,\\frac{{s}^{2}+13}{{e}^{s}}⟩.$" "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=⟨4{s}^{7}+13,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,\\frac{{s}^{2}+13}{{e}^{s}}⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186368032 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nAll four sample slopes are already computed. So, we just need to find the increment on $y.$\n\nWe compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(1+2\\cdot \\frac{9}{4}+2\\cdot \\frac{51}{16}+\\frac{67}{8})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{81}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $\\frac{51}{16}$ | $\\frac{67}{8}$ | $\\frac{27}{8}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\frac{27}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $\\frac{51}{16}$ | $\\frac{67}{8}$ | $\\frac{27}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{35}{8}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1)≈\\frac{35}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nAll four sample slopes are already computed. So, we just need to find the increment on $y.$\n\nWe compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(1+2\\cdot \\frac{9}{4}+2\\cdot \\frac{51}{16}+\\frac{67}{8})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{81}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $\\frac{51}{16}$ | $\\frac{67}{8}$ | $\\frac{27}{8}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\frac{27}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $\\frac{51}{16}$ | $\\frac{67}{8}$ | $\\frac{27}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{35}{8}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1)≈\\frac{35}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186368062 :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{9t}q(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{9t}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-9)t}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $Q(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-9)t}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t=Q(s-9),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-9>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+9.\n$$" "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{9t}q(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{9t}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-9)t}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $$\n\\displaystyle Q(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-9)t}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t=Q(s-9),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-9>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+9.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186368108 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ {y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}2x & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x) & 2y\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(1,1) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2(1) & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (1)) \\\\ -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (1)) & 2(1)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\text{almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$" "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ {y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}2x & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x) & 2y\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(1,1) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2(1) & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (1)) \\\\ -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (1)) & 2(1)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\text{almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186368125 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ {y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y))=\\pi x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-1)=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-1)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) & \\pi x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x) & 2y\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) & \\pi (0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) & 2(0)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is zero, our system is $\\text{not almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$" "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ {y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y))=\\pi x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-1)=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-1)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) & \\pi x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x) & 2y\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,0) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) & \\pi (0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) & 2(0)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is zero, our system is $\\text{not almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186368175 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+2y-6 \\\\ x-3y+{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2y-6)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2y-6)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-3y+{y}^{2})=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-3y+{y}^{2})=-3+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2y-3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2(2) & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2(1)-3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x-2 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+2y-6 \\\\ x-3y+{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2y-6)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2y-6)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-3y+{y}^{2})=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-3y+{y}^{2})=-3+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2y-3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2(2) & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2(1)-3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x-2 \\\\ y-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186368184 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+xy-2x+3y+5 \\\\ {y}^{2}-x+4y+4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+xy-2x+3y+5)=2x+y-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xy-2x+3y+5)=x+3 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-x+4y+4)=-1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-x+4y+4)=2y+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+y-2 & x+3 \\\\ -1 & 2y+4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2(1)+(-1)-2 & 1+3 \\\\ -1 & 2(-1)+4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+xy-2x+3y+5 \\\\ {y}^{2}-x+4y+4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+xy-2x+3y+5)=2x+y-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xy-2x+3y+5)=x+3 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-x+4y+4)=-1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-x+4y+4)=2y+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+y-2 & x+3 \\\\ -1 & 2y+4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2(1)+(-1)-2 & 1+3 \\\\ -1 & 2(-1)+4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186368193 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1) \\\\ 1-{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1))=\\frac{1}{y+1} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1))=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1))=-\\frac{1}{y+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) & \\frac{1}{y+1} \\\\ -2x & -\\frac{1}{y+1}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )^{2}+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-1\\pm \\text{i}\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nFinally, since we have two complex eigenvalues with negative real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$" "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1) \\\\ 1-{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1))=\\frac{1}{y+1} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1))=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1))=-\\frac{1}{y+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) & \\frac{1}{y+1} \\\\ -2x & -\\frac{1}{y+1}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), so the system is almost linear at ${x}^{∗}.$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )^{2}+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-1\\pm \\text{i}\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nFinally, since we have two complex eigenvalues with negative real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186368201 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+y+{x}^{3} \\\\ -2x+{y}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =2+3{x}^{2},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2+3{x}^{2} & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2+0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=1\\pm i.$\n\nSince we have two complex eigenvalues with positive real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{spiral source}.$" "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x+y+{x}^{3} \\\\ -2x+{y}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =2+3{x}^{2},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =-2,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2+3{x}^{2} & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}2+0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=1\\pm i.$\n\nSince we have two complex eigenvalues with positive real parts, our equilibrium is an $\\text{unstable}$ $\\text{spiral source}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186368209 :question/problem "Given that $D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+2,$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=2{x}^{3}-x+1?$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+2,\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=2{x}^{3}-x+1?$"] [:db/cas 17592186368351 :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $6$-periodic function $(L=3)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right))+x(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right):$\n\n$$\n[-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A &=0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\pi }{3}B &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=0 \\\\ B &=\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\frac{3x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$" "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $6$-periodic function $(L=3)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right))+x(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation $$\n\\displaystyle {y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right):\n$$\n\n$$\n[-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A &=0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\pi }{3}B &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=0 \\\\ B &=\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\frac{3x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186368362 :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right))+x(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n[-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\pi A &=4 \\\\ \\pi B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=-\\frac{4}{\\pi } \\\\ B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=-\\frac{4x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$" "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right))+x(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation $$\n\\displaystyle {y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right):\n$$\n\n$$\n[-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\pi A &=4 \\\\ \\pi B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=-\\frac{4}{\\pi } \\\\ B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=-\\frac{4x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186368382 :question/problem "A body of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons. At time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}$ meters.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{s}$" "A body of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons. At time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $$\n\\displaystyle s(t)=\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}\n$$ meters.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{s}$"] [:db/cas 17592186368391 :question/problem "Find the area $A$ of the finite region bounded between the curve $x=\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{5}-\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}$ and the $y$-axis for $y\\ge 0.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" "Find the area $A$ of the finite region bounded between the curve $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{5}-\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}\n$$ and the $y$-axis for $y\\ge 0.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186368391 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{5}-\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{y}^{3}({y}^{2}-y-6) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{y}^{3}(y+2)(y-3) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,\\,0,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region:\n\n![](images/8b/8bc9294352d263d7ff57eb257dae9a584b47927b18268ed0ddbe13ba0e2141ce.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x=\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{5}-\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=0$ to $y=3.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{5}-\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y-\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y-\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{6}}{6}\\,|}_{0}^{3}-\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}\\,|}_{0}^{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}\\,|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{243}{2}-0)-\\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{243}{5}-0)-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{81}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}-\\frac{27}{5}-\\frac{27}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{27}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So, the area that we want is\n\n$$\nA=|-\\frac{27}{5}|=\\frac{27}{5}.\n$$" "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{5}-\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{y}^{3}({y}^{2}-y-6) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{y}^{3}(y+2)(y-3) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,\\,0,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region:\n\n![](images/8b/8bc9294352d263d7ff57eb257dae9a584b47927b18268ed0ddbe13ba0e2141ce.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{5}-\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}\n$$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=0$ to $y=3.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{5}-\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y-\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y-\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{6}}{6}\\,|}_{0}^{3}-\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}\\,|}_{0}^{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}\\,|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{243}{2}-0)-\\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{243}{5}-0)-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{81}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}-\\frac{27}{5}-\\frac{27}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{27}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So, the area that we want is\n\n$$\nA=|-\\frac{27}{5}|=\\frac{27}{5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186368397 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /16}({cos}^{2}⁡(4x)-{sin}^{2}⁡(4x))\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /16}({cos}^{2}⁡(4x)-{sin}^{2}⁡(4x))\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186368428 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{25}\\frac{1}{25+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{arctan}⁡($\n{{field-2}}$)$" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{25}\\frac{1}{25+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{arctan}⁡($\n{{field-2}}$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186368541 :question/problem "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$H=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle H=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186368575 :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$" "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $$\n\\displaystyle v=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩\n$$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$"] [:db/cas 17592186368575 :question/worked-solution "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{1},-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩.$ \nSubstituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{C}_{1},-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩ & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+{C}_{1},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$" "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{1},-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩.\n$$ \nSubstituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{C}_{1},-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩ & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+{C}_{1},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186368608 :question/problem "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation ${y}^{′}-6y=0.$ \n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=0,1,2,…?$" "Let $$\n\\displaystyle y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}\n$$ be a series solution to the equation ${y}^{′}-6y=0.$ \n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=0,1,2,…?$"] [:db/cas 17592186368716 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{10}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{10}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186368730 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{t\\to 0}‖f(t)‖$, if $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}{t} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{-4t}-1}{t} \\\\ \\frac{2{t}^{2}-8}{t-1}\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Calculate ${lim}_{t\\to 0}‖f(t)‖$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}{t} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{-4t}-1}{t} \\\\ \\frac{2{t}^{2}-8}{t-1}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186368784 :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{y}{x} & \\frac{x}{y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{y}{x})=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{1}{x} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(e,e),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(e,e)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{e} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{e}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $H(e,e)>0$ is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(e,e),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(e,e)=det({f}^{″}(e,e))=\\frac{1}{e}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{e}\\right)-{0}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(e,e)>0$ is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(e,e)=({f}^{″}(e,e))_{11}=\\frac{1}{e}>0.$\n\n- Statement “The point $(e,e)$ is a saddle point of $f$” is true. Indeed, since $H(e,e)<0,$ the point $(e,e)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(e,e)>0$; “The point $(e,e)$ is a saddle point of $f$” only." "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{y}{x} & \\frac{x}{y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-\\frac{y}{x})=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{1}{x} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(e,e),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(e,e)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{e} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{e}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $H(e,e)>0$ is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(e,e),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(e,e)=det({f}^{″}(e,e))=\\frac{1}{e}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{e}\\right)-{0}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(e,e)>0$ is true. Indeed, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(e,e)=({f}^{″}(e,e))_{11}=\\frac{1}{e}>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The point $(e,e)$ is a saddle point of $f$” is true. Indeed, since $H(e,e)<0,$ the point $(e,e)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(e,e)>0$; “The point $(e,e)$ is a saddle point of $f$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186368927 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the polar curve $r=5-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the following identity: $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}.$*" "Find the arc length of the polar curve $r=5-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the following identity: $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}.\n$$*"] [:db/cas 17592186368962 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186368969 :question/worked-solution "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{6}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{x-5}]}_{6}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-5}-2\\sqrt{6-5}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-5}-2) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}\\,{x}^{-2/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{x}^{1/3}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt[3]{a}-1) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals ${\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x$ and ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x$ are divergent." "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{6}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{x-5}]}_{6}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-5}-2\\sqrt{6-5}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-5}-2) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}\\,{x}^{-2/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{x}^{1/3}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt[3]{a}-1) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-5}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ are divergent."] [:db/cas 17592186368983 :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)}{4}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =-\\frac{1}{4}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})}{4}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{4}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is also  $\\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{m/s}.$" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)}{4}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =-\\frac{1}{4}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $$\n\\displaystyle v(t)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})}{4}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{4}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is also  $\\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{m/s}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186369119 :question/problem "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186369126 :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186369189 :question/worked-solution "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $D$ the diameter of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the diameter $D$ of the circle. Since $D=2r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $D$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {\\left(\\frac{D}{2}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}D}\\left(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & =\\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ D & =\\frac{4}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $D=2r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2r & =\\frac{4}{5\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $D$ the diameter of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the diameter $D$ of the circle. Since $D=2r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $D$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {\\left(\\frac{D}{2}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}D}\\left(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & =\\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ D & =\\frac{4}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $D=2r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2r & =\\frac{4}{5\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186369210 :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the sphere, $V$ the volume of the sphere, and $A$ its surface area. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the volume $V$ and the surface area $A$ of the sphere. Since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2}$ and $V=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3},$ the relationship between $V$ and $A$ is\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{\\left(\\frac{A}{4\\pi }\\right)}^{3/2}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{3(4\\pi )^{1/2}}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}A}\\left(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }} \\\\ {A}^{1/2} & =12\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\ A & ={(12\\sqrt{\\pi })}^{2} \\\\ A & =144\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\pi {r}^{2} & =144\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =36 \\\\ r & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $r$ be the radius of the sphere, $V$ the volume of the sphere, and $A$ its surface area. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the volume $V$ and the surface area $A$ of the sphere. Since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2}$ and $V=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3},$ the relationship between $V$ and $A$ is\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{\\left(\\frac{A}{4\\pi }\\right)}^{3/2}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{3(4\\pi )^{1/2}}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}A}\\left(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},\n$$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }} \\\\ {A}^{1/2} & =12\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\ A & ={(12\\sqrt{\\pi })}^{2} \\\\ A & =144\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\pi {r}^{2} & =144\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =36 \\\\ r & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186369217 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(2-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+18{y}^{2}=36,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(2-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+18{y}^{2}=36,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?"] [:db/cas 17592186369224 :question/problem "![](images/f7/f74113e44d4087a1aa705779b1908341bd580851e98ecda7cf06499188ad4158.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{2y}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the quarter of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=18,$ located in the first quadrant, shown above?" "![](images/f7/f74113e44d4087a1aa705779b1908341bd580851e98ecda7cf06499188ad4158.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{2y}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is the quarter of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=18,$ located in the first quadrant, shown above?"] [:db/cas 17592186369231 :question/problem "![](images/ff/ff5883b08bdd339549d03542b87da900ab014954bc52764d0e5da087a72c1e95.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral ${\\int }_{C}xy\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+81}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the quarter of the ellipse $25{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=225,$ located in the third quadrant, shown above?" "![](images/ff/ff5883b08bdd339549d03542b87da900ab014954bc52764d0e5da087a72c1e95.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}xy\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+81}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is the quarter of the ellipse $25{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=225,$ located in the third quadrant, shown above?"] [:db/cas 17592186369258 :question/problem "![](images/f4/f402fe97e6f76cd94b94331e7f30ddb09c978c93b86ce112a5232042a2f741cc.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}2y\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36,$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(-2,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" "![](images/f4/f402fe97e6f76cd94b94331e7f30ddb09c978c93b86ce112a5232042a2f741cc.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}2y\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36,$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(-2,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186369265 :question/problem "![](images/b2/b21c9c11aa3320a729db0d8a71f5c89069d46527e78dfc76b47ae3925387c770.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(3x+2y)\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-2,2)$ to the point $(-1,1),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨t-2,\\,2-t⟩.$*" "![](images/b2/b21c9c11aa3320a729db0d8a71f5c89069d46527e78dfc76b47ae3925387c770.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(3x+2y)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-2,2)$ to the point $(-1,1),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨t-2,\\,2-t⟩.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186369272 :question/problem "![](images/be/be3ad044a611d99e67c89d053d4878a2835be65828a4dc9300697bfd61822994.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the below-left quarter of the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ traversed from the point $(-1,0)$ to the point $(0,-2),$ as shown above." "![](images/be/be3ad044a611d99e67c89d053d4878a2835be65828a4dc9300697bfd61822994.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the below-left quarter of the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ traversed from the point $(-1,0)$ to the point $(0,-2),$ as shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186369298 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$F$ is **not** conservative” is false. The vector field $F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is false. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\n- Statement “${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne 0$ for some closed paths $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is false. The circulation $Γ=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$F$ is **not** conservative” is false. The vector field $F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is false. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne 0\n$$ for some closed paths $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is false. The circulation $Γ=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""] [:db/cas 17592186369305 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is true. The vector field $F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any piecewise linear path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is false, while statement “${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$F$ is conservative”; “${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” only." "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is true. The vector field $F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ for any piecewise linear path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is false, while statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$F$ is conservative”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186369312 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=x{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is true. The vector field $F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement “${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any ellipse $C\\in {R}^{2}$” and “${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” are true. The circulation $Γ=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$F$ is conservative”; “${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any ellipse $C\\in {R}^{2}$”; “${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$”." "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=x{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is true. The vector field $F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ for any ellipse $C\\in {R}^{2}$” and “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” are true. The circulation $Γ=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$F$ is conservative”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ for any ellipse $C\\in {R}^{2}$”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$”."] [:db/cas 17592186369345 :question/worked-solution "The coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}$ is given by $\\frac{{f}^{(4)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{4!}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{(4)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{4!} & =\\frac{1}{24} \\\\ \\frac{{f}^{(4)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{24} & =\\frac{1}{24} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}$ is given by $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{f}^{(4)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{4!}.\n$$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{(4)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{4!} & =\\frac{1}{24} \\\\ \\frac{{f}^{(4)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{24} & =\\frac{1}{24} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186369366 :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=⟨0,\\,x⟩$ give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Pair $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y},\\,y⟩$ does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{y}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{e}^{y}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair $F(x,y)=⟨-\\frac{y}{5}+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,\\,\\frac{4x}{5}⟩$ gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{5}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{4}{5},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F(x,y)=⟨0,\\,x⟩$; $F(x,y)=⟨-\\frac{y}{5}+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,\\,\\frac{4x}{5}⟩$ only." "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=⟨0,\\,x⟩$ give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Pair $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y},\\,y⟩$ does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{y}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{e}^{y}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨-\\frac{y}{5}+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,\\,\\frac{4x}{5}⟩\n$$ gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{5}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{4}{5},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F(x,y)=⟨0,\\,x⟩$; $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨-\\frac{y}{5}+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,\\,\\frac{4x}{5}⟩\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186369434 :question/problem "![](images/39/393bed476f2e8d84fc7e5dc721d77749f7f259834e03a94a48a08533ae9befa6.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}2y\\,dx+4x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above." "![](images/39/393bed476f2e8d84fc7e5dc721d77749f7f259834e03a94a48a08533ae9befa6.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}2y\\,dx+4x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186369441 :question/problem "![](images/6b/6b52e4f2f37d6ba6971fbc8f6e1d610ab7e0c9d11341ea3b6a6772bff8f40e37.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}3y\\,dx+2x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." "![](images/6b/6b52e4f2f37d6ba6971fbc8f6e1d610ab7e0c9d11341ea3b6a6772bff8f40e37.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}3y\\,dx+2x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186369455 :question/problem "![](im